ow going to  write me a letter saying this is the way  this is going to work we're going to  have a process I'm asking for  ministerial involvement and monitoring  of that he's not going to negotiate with  me that's not his job I understand that  but he's got to put some momentum into  this process so that we can get a  settlement and hopefully call off these  strikes but we won't call them off until  we've got tangible progress and a  document we can put to our members in a  referendum yeah I mean obviously as you  say you're not you're not directly  negotiating with the transport secretary  it's it's with the network Rail and the  train companies do you feel like there's  a a strategy of delaying tactics and it  might not well be their priority  absolutely we the last time we had a  strike was eight weeks ago and they we  postponed a set of strike two weeks ago  or two and a half weeks ago in order to  have intensive negotiations we got in to  the end of that two-week process and  they produce nothing Network rail didn't  give us a new position and the train  operating companies gave us nothing at  all I haven't even got a document to  give to my members and say what do you  think of this so that's unacceptable so  he needs to step in now and clarify what  the roles of his agents are that's the  train operators and network Rail and  allow them to make serious offers in  order to get progress in this dispute  let's talk about the people most  affected by this and that's ordinary  people trying to get to work are you  holding the general public to Ransom now  no that's just a cliche that people pick  up you know this is worthy of the Daily  Mail which isn't very worthy at all so  people throw those cliches at us we live  in these communities we live amongst the  people that are affected we don't want  to affect them my members lose their  wages when they go on strike the public  is inconvenienced the businesses that  we're hearing about in hospitality and  so on are losing money  with Christmas coming you know who  doesn't lose money the train operators  they are indemnified every time we go on  strike for 30 million pounds the  Department of Transport points in  Striking if it's not available  50 million pounds on subsidizing this  strike it would have been far cheaper to  settle that strike on our complete terms  at the beginning it's a ridiculous  situation which shows you that we've had  an incompetent government three  different transport secretaries a dozen  transport under secretaries and God  knows what's going on in the background  over there while they're fighting it out  what they can't do is step up to their  responsibilities to the public and to  the industry which they're meant to  control briefly because we're running  out of time and I've got two more  questions I want to ask you before my  producer tells me to move on first we're  in a cost of living crisis people are  really struggling everyone I I know and  grown up with are struggling right now  what do you say to the person who's  who's a daily wager and if they don't  get to work on time and they can't  afford a taxi to get there and you're  striking what do you say to them well  the buses aren't working the the  underground is working at the moment we  don't want this inconvenience what I  would say to those people who are in  precarious employment created by this  government and the laws the poor  employment laws is that they need a  strong union and we need a better deal  at the workplace what they want to do to  our members is put us into those  precarious situations through  subcontracting and Outsourcing we're  sorry for that but the British worker  needs a better deal they need a better  deal on employment right and let's talk  about that  very briefly  um you've started a bit of a tsunami  haven't you as far as strikes are  concerned well we're glad that people  have picked up on our message that  working-class people deserve respect and  dignity in the workplace we used to have  very firm contracts of employment and  companies that would give undertakings  that they would carry through in terms  of sick pay holidays pensions even  social and Welfare facilities that's all  been Stripped Away and we need to go  back to that so that people feel secure  in their communities and securing their  employment and we can create a better  economy and a better Society on that  basis not everyone needs to be  outsourced subcontracted and vulnerable  but that's what they want to do to  British workers across our society and  across our economy in pursuit of profit  and in an answer are you all  coordinating now all the unions talking  and trying well we talk to each other  yes and it would be very sensible for  unions to coordinate because the themes  are the same the themes are driving up  profits and driving down wages and  conditions so Unions would be dafted if  they didn't talk to each other about  Effectiveness and coordination Nick  Lynch General Secretary of the national  Union of rail Maritime and transport  workers thanks very much indeed for  coming in tonight thank you
 

